<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Historical Sites – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Historical Sites and Tourism</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> Our Historical Sites</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Summarize articles for classroom discussions. Concept: Explain cultural differences.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain cultural differences by using pictures of traditional artifacts and gestures (e.g., points to a handmade Ngäbe-Buglé chácara and explains how it is woven by hand, holds up a Guna Yala mola and describes its vibrant patterns and stories, or shows an Emberá basket and explains how it is crafted from natural fibers like palm leaves).
            Can explain Indigenous cultural differences using visuals and gestures (e.g., shows a picture of traditional clothing and gestures to highlight its colors).
            Can summarize key points from an article about indigenous cultures to help peers understand important traditions and practices during classroom discussions (e.g., creating a mind map about how the Naso Tjër Di celebrate their festival).
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on engaging students, activating prior knowledge, modeling target language, and clarifying concepts necessary for the lesson's mediation tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images of various Panamanian historical sites (e.g., Panama Canal, Casco Viejo, Portobelo fort, Panama Viejo ruins) and indigenous artifacts (e.g., Guna Yala mola, Ngäbe-Buglé chácara). Students, in pairs, briefly describe what they see and what they remember about each. The teacher asks, "If you had to explain one of these to a tourist who knows nothing about Panama, which one would you choose and what main ideas would you tell them?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models explaining a Guna Yala mola using a visual. "This is a Guna Yala mola. It is a traditional handmade textile art made by the Guna women. Molas are famous for their vibrant patterns and stories. They often depict animals, plants, or abstract designs. A tourist is going to find them very attractive because they represent Panama's rich cultural heritage." The teacher highlights how to summarize key facts and explain cultural significance using descriptive adjectives and simple sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I explain something like the mola, do I need to tell every single detail, or just the most important parts?" (Just the most important parts.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a tourist doesn't speak much English, how can I help them understand cultural differences?" (Use simple words, gestures, pictures.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive new language input related to historical sites and indigenous cultures, presented in an authentic context to prepare them for mediation tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Historical Sites and Tourism":</b> The teacher provides a short informational article (approx. 150-200 words) from a tourist website about the cultural practices of the Emberá indigenous group, focusing on their basket weaving and connection to nature. The article includes vocabulary like <i>heritage, preserve, craft, natural fibers, destination, visitor</i>, and uses present simple/past simple to describe traditions and their history.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An authentic narrative excerpt/informational article titled "The Art of the Emberá: A Journey Through Tradition" from a cultural tourism blog. The genre is informative, and the register is tourist-friendly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding intent and context: "The tone of this article is informative because it provides detailed historical facts about Casco Viejo." (Teacher guides students to identify the tone of the Emberá article). Also, students will prepare to participate in conversations by summarizing the article's content.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main idea of this article about the Emberá people?" (To inform about their cultural traditions, especially basket weaving).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Emberá baskets are made from plastic." (False) "The Emberá people try to preserve their traditions." (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match key phrases from the article (e.g., "palm leaves," "ancient techniques," "sustainable practices") with simple definitions or images.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on developing accuracy in mediating information using target grammar, vocabulary, and pragmatic functions. Students will practice summarizing and explaining cultural differences.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present simple (describing traditions), simple past (historical context of crafts), possessive 's (Emberá's crafts), future tense with "going to" (explaining what visitors are going to experience).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> <i>heritage, preserve, craft, natural fibers, destination, visitor, attract, describe, inform, cultural, historical.</i></li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice stress in multisyllabic words like "culture," "cultural," "history," "historical," "inform," and "information" while summarizing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Summarizing key information from the article. Comparing/contrasting different aspects of indigenous crafts (e.g., "Emberá baskets use natural fibers, while Guna molas are fabric").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: "Summary Sentence Match":</b> In pairs, students receive scrambled sentences related to the Emberá article's main points and simpler, summarized versions. They must match the original sentence to its simplified summary. For example: "The Emberá utilize various natural materials like chunga palm leaves to create their intricate baskets" matches "Emberá people use palm leaves to make baskets."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: "Explain the Artifact":</b> Students work in small groups. The teacher provides pictures of different indigenous artifacts (e.g., an Emberá basket, a Ngäbe-Buglé chácara, a Guna mola). Each group chooses one picture. They must write 3-4 sentences to explain their chosen artifact, summarizing its origin, materials, and cultural importance to a new visitor. They are prompted to use phrases like "This is [artifact]. It is famous because..." and "Visitors are going to learn about..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction/Peer Check: "Mini-Mediator":</b> Students partner up. Student A explains their chosen artifact from the previous activity to Student B, using gestures and the sentences they prepared. Student B listens, takes notes on the key points, and then asks one clarifying question. They swap roles. Student B provides feedback on how clearly Student A summarized and explained, focusing on intelligibility and use of target vocabulary/grammar.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students apply their mediation skills in a realistic communicative task, developing a section of their "Digital Tourist Guide" and explaining its content.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Historical Sites and Tourism":</b> Students work in their "Digital Tourist Guide" project groups (established in previous lessons). Each group selects one historical site or indigenous cultural aspect/artifact from their guide that they have already researched. Their task is to act as tour guides and *mediate* the information for a hypothetical group of international tourists (represented by other students or the teacher).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Short, interactive presentation (3-4 minutes) where students present a specific section of their Digital Tourist Guide. They must summarize key facts about the site/artifact and explain its cultural significance, using visuals from their digital guide. They should focus on making complex information accessible and engaging for tourists.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connect directly to Theme: "Our Historical Sites" and Scenario: "Historical Sites and Tourism":</b> For example, one group might explain the historical importance of Casco Viejo, using simple past ("It was a vibrant colonial village") and present simple ("Today, it attracts many visitors"). Another group might explain the Ngäbe-Buglé culture's tradition of weaving chácaras, explaining how it is woven by hand and what it represents, using pictures and gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in explaining, clarity in summarizing, use of appropriate vocabulary and grammar for descriptive and informative purposes, collaborative interaction within the group, and effective use of visuals/gestures to mediate understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage assesses students' ability to mediate information effectively and provides opportunities for self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on the clarity of explanations, use of target language, and effective mediation. Peer assessment can also be incorporated, where 'tourists' (peers) evaluate how well the information was explained.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their chosen section of the Digital Tourist Guide. The teacher observes their ability to:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarize key historical facts or cultural traditions from their research.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explain cultural differences or historical importance to their 'tourist' audience.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use visuals and gestures to enhance understanding.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation and appropriate stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "cultural," "historical").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate and relevant use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>historical sites, heritage, monument, preserve, attract, visitor</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of grammar (present simple/simple past for descriptions, 'going to' for tourist plans, possessive 's).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and conciseness in summarizing information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effectiveness in explaining cultural differences or historical context, using visuals and gestures (as per learning outcome).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative participation and support within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick reflection sheet:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about explaining historical facts or cultural differences to your 'tourist' peers?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using pictures or gestures help you make your explanation clearer?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage encourages both student and teacher to reflect on the learning process and performance, informing future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which part of our Digital Tourist Guide project did you find most interesting to explain to others, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new vocabulary or grammar did you use most effectively today to explain something clearly to your peers?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students mediate information and explain cultural differences? The use of visual aids and structured practice activities.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future mediation tasks? Perhaps more practice with paraphrasing complex sentences into simpler language for A2.4 learners.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the Digital Tourist Guide project effectively integrate the mediation skill? Yes, it provided a real-world context for explaining and summarizing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Common areas for improvement identified: Some students still struggle with appropriate pronunciation of multisyllabic words, and some summaries were too detailed.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "Our Historical Sites" and the Digital Tourist Guide project. The next theme will introduce new vocabulary and scenarios, building upon the communicative skills developed in this unit. Students' digital guides will be shared with the school community to celebrate their learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>